Interface#h1

Ecmascript module loader for clients/imports/functions. 

2024

{text-align:left;margin-left:10%;}
  The web has become professionally unmaintainable. 

  This module paves a way out, the case is made in my notes on Form@#Blik_2024_form. 

{text-align:left;margin-left:10%;}
  The javascript platform has been increasingly convoluted 
by non-standard syntactic variations in its ecosystem (commonjs, typescript, flow, jsx &c.), 
requiring growing amounts of configuration to maintain (manifest, tsconfig, webpack/babel, eslint &c.). 

  Even when they have merit (static analysis of type integrity, templating), 
it is at the expense of javascript's flexibility, which can indeed offer 
native alternatives to these features. 

  As insult to injury, package managers (npm, yarn, jsr &c.) 
have long obscured dependencies and build processes of external libraries, 
contributing to the overengineering and growing complexity 
on the web by concealing implementation details in a language 
that is ultimately still distributed in source format (so to browsers as to servers). 

{text-align:center;}
(Programmming should make us creative, not complacent.)#b 
{text-align:left;}

With the arrival of a loader interface in the (Ecmascript specification)@https://whatwg.github.io/loader/{"class":"invert reference","style":{"& *":{"filter":"invert(0.8)"}}} and its (Node.js implementation)@https://nodejs.org/api/module.html#customization-hooks, 
variations can finally be standardized dynamically, without a need for external configurations 
or a new platform trying to contain this heterogeneity (UMD ✞@https://jameshfisher.com/2020/10/04/what-are-umd-modules/, Deno@https://www.youtube.com/embed/M3BM9TB-8yA, Bun@https://bun.sh/docs). 

(Loading modules)#b requires 3 steps: 

 (1. locate)#b (download, bundle and delete source if not found despite source definition specified, or just redirect to the source entry if available (eg. pending or interrupted bundling). exposed by nodejs as "resolve".), 
 (2. access)#b (parse and re-serialize if foreign dialect specified or detected. exposed by nodejs as "load".), 
 (3. modularise)#b (not exposed by nodejs; splitting "load" into access + modularise with an implementation using the "vm" module is a pending feature). 
 (+. test)#b, if the namespace exposes cases for itself. 

Although the language allows polluting modules with procedures, it is advised to keep them modular and generic. 
To invoke their functions with specific arguments, this module 
extends the 3 steps with an inference of arguments provided by a client (shell/function): 

 (5. infer arguments)#b if provided. 

Take a simple module with a function to cumulate numbers: 
 sum#script("/Blik_2023_search.js/scope/sum"){background:transparent;max-width:90%;margin:auto}

To invoke it, you shouldn't need to write a static script for each specific case of any module. 
Interface will dynamically infer the function name and arguments provided from a command line: (
 git clone https://github.com/bpstrngr/interface .; 
 node ./Blik_2023_interface.js ./Blik_2023_search.js sum 1 2 3;)#span{font-family:monospace;color:#43a047} 
or peer module: inference#script/highlight(
' import("./Blik_2023_interface.js").then(({resolve})=>
 resolve("./Blik_2023_search.js","sum",1,2,3));'
){font-family:monospace;}
When no inference is provided, the functions will be exposed in a REPL: (
 node ./Blik_2023_interface.js;
)#span{font-family:monospace;color:#43a047} inference#script/highlight(
' resolve("./Blik_2023_search.js","sum",1,2,3);'
){font-family:monospace;}

To use Interface internally for all module imports, specify it as node.js's loader module (available from version 16): (
 # module detection fails to detect eg. modules with export statements far in the file, so package.json is recommended after version 20.7 too. 
 node --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js ./Blik_2023_search.js sum 1 2 3; # node.js 22.7.0 deprecated commonjs syntax detection by default. 
 node --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js --experimental-detect-module ./Blik_2023_search.js sum 1 2 3; # node.js 21.1.0
 node --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js ./Blik_2023_search.js sum 1 2 3; # node.js 20.7.0, with a package.json determining module defaults (echo '{"type":"module"}'>package.json)
 node --import 'data:text/javascript;import("module").then(({register})=>register("./Blik_2023_interface.js",{parentURL:pathToFileURL("./")}));' ./Blik_2023_search.js sum 1 2 3; # node.js 20.7.0
 # node.js 20.2 may have broken access to command line arguments from a worker thread used by both --loader and its new --import flag. 
 node --loader=./Blik_2023_interface.js ./Blik_2023_search.js sum 1 2 3; # node.js 16
 node -e 'let{interface,inference}=Promise.all(["interface","inference"].map(m=>import("../Blik_2023_"+m+".js"))).then(m=>{[interface,inference]=m;[{resolve},{infer,compose,combine}]=m;resolve(...process.argv.slice(1))})';)#div{width:100%;overflow:scroll;white-space:pre;font-family:monospace;color:#43a047;} 

By default, node.js rejects module (and npm package) specifiers that aren't available, requiring pre-installation of dependencies (and dealing with npm version conflicts). 
Interface recovers from failed imports if a respective entry is declared in source definitions (sources.json), 
loading and accessing them as specified there, while also bundling them as standard ecmascript modules 
to dispose of arbitrary architectures for future access: 
definition#script/highlight(
` // sources.json
 {"Blik_2023_search.js":
 {"https://github.com/bpstrngr/interface":
 {"2024.02":["Blik_2023_search.js"]
 }
 }
 }
`)

Source definitions map relative file specifiers to local/remote entry points (address and branch, supporting multi-entry and sparse-checkout of monorepos), 
patches and parsing/bundling specifications (syntax, alias, edit (source), replace (bundle)): 
(rollup options and plugin specifiers (output:{paths:{specifier:"path"}}, plugin:{option:"settings"}) are now obsolete, along with rollup itself once syntax merging is implemented): 
definitions#script/highlight(
` {"module.js":"path/index.js"};
 {"module.js":["path/index.js",{syntax:"commonjs",edit:{regexp:"string"},alias:{trail:{specifier:"specifier"}},replace:{syntax:{value:"value"}}}]};
 {"module.js":{"protocol://domain/author/module":["index.js","index2.js"]}};
 {"module.js":{"protocol://domain/author/module/tarball/branch":["path/index.js"]}};
 {"module.js":{"protocol://domain/author/module":{"branch or tag":["path/index.js","../../module.patch"]}}};
 {"module.js":{"protocol://domain/author/module/sparse/checkout/path sparse/checkout/path":{"branch or tag":["path/index.js"]}}};
`)

Let's see a source definitions for the syntactic parser, formatter and bundler Interface uses to achieve standardization: 
 sum#script("/Blik_2023_sources.json/scope/default/haverbeke_2012_acorn.js"){background:transparent;max-width:90%;margin:auto}
 sum#script("/Blik_2023_sources.json/scope/default/davidbonnet_2015_astring.js"){background:transparent;max-width:90%;margin:auto}
 sum#script("/Blik_2023_sources.json/scope/default/Harris_2015_rollup.js"){background:transparent;max-width:90%;margin:auto;opacity:0.8}

When importing these the first time, they will perform parsing and bundling 
of their own source by being loaded from right there, before replacing 
their arbitrarily structured sources with an idempotent bundle. 

They will be imported no matter what missing source with a definition you may load first: (
 node --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js ./haverbeke_2012_acorn.js;)#span{font-family:monospace;color:#43a047} 

To perform a full resolution of external modules, iterate through the source definition entries: (
 for file in $(cat sources.json | jq keys[] --raw-output);do node ./Blik_2023_interface.js --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js $file;done;)#div{width:100%;overflow:scroll;white-space:pre;font-family:monospace;color:#43a047;}
or resolve only static imports by loading source modules to avoid dynamic ones: (
 for file in $(git ls-tree --full-tree --name-only -r HEAD);do node ./Blik_2023_interface.js --import=./Blik_2023_interface.js $file;done;)#div{width:100%;overflow:scroll;white-space:pre;font-family:monospace;color:#43a047;}

Due to lack of loader, git or vm modules, browser clients can only perform location, access and testing, not modularization: clientside#script/highlight(
` either(resolve,compose(fetch,"text",definition,interpret,definition,standardize,serialize,"body",specify,{method:"put"},merge,infer.bind(module),fetch,resolve))(module);`)

But that's also all you'd need to do on a server to trigger it by responding with the accessed source file. 

{text-align:center;}
(Be the host of your property.)#h2 
{text-align:left;}


Now we can consider something potentially more demanding than cumulating numbers, 
like opening a port to exchange http messages: 
 host#script("/Blik_2023_host.js/scope/expose"){background:transparent;max-width:90%;height:12em;overflow:scroll;margin:auto}

As visible, this implementation takes a protocol configuration file, 
which is recorded in a list of classified files, 
along with the addresses of SSL certificates it may contain. 
If the certification is not found to host over https, the distinguishedname is used to generate a self-signed one: definitions#script/highlight(
` {"http":{"port":80}
 ,"https":{"port":443,"hmac":"encryption seed"
 ,"certification":{"domain name":
["./certificate_key.pem","./certificate.pem"
]}
 ,"distinguishedname":{"commonName":"example.org","countryName":"US","ST":"California","localityName":"Mountain View","organizationName":"example","OU":"admin"}
 }
 }
`)

 host#script("/Blik_2024_static.js/scope/default"){background:transparent;max-width:90%;height:12em;overflow:scroll;margin:auto}


{text-align:center;}
("Composition over convention")#b 
